      </div>
       <div data-template-id="targets-panel" class="canva-panel panel mt-6">
        <h3 data-template-id="targets-heading" style="font-size:18.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text mb-3">Learning Targets</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 marker:text-purple-700">
         <li data-template-id="target-one" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text">I can identify how customer insight influenced an entrepreneurial decision.</li>
         <li data-template-id="target-two" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text">I can communicate an entrepreneurial idea through purposeful visual choices.</li>
         <li data-template-id="target-three" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text">I can use evidence from a peer test to improve my work.</li>
        </ul>
       </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="case-heading">
       <div class="section-title">
        <span class="icon-badge" aria-hidden="true"><i data-lucide="search-check"></i></span>
        <h2 id="case-heading" data-template-id="case-heading" style="font-size:23.9993px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text">Case Study: Gymshark and Customer Insight</h2>
       </div>
       <p data-template-id="reading-focus" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(104, 99, 108)" class="canva-text italic mb-5">Reading focus: Resourcefulness and customer insight</p>
       <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[1fr_220px] gap-6 items-start">
        <div class="case-copy">
         <p><strong>The opening.</strong> In the early 2010s, Ben Francis was a university student in Birmingham, England. He delivered pizzas, studied, and spent much of his free time lifting weights with friends. He wanted to enter the fitness industry, so he tried building workout apps and a fitness social network. Those projects attracted some users, but they did not become sustainable businesses.</p>
         <p><strong>The first test.</strong> Francis started Gymshark in 2012 as a website with very little money and no inventory. The site first used drop shipping: when a customer placed an order, another retailer shipped the product, and Gymshark earned a small margin. The first order took about two months. This approach was limited, but it let the team test whether customers would buy through the site before spending heavily on stock.</p>
         <p><strong>The customer problem.</strong> The team soon noticed that existing gym clothing did not match what they wanted to wear. Many items felt baggy, rigid, or designed for a different type of lifter. Because Francis and his friends were members of the lifting community, they experienced the frustration themselves and understood the style, fit, and function their peers cared about. They began making fitted, lightweight clothing with a sewing machine and screen printer. Francis helped sew, print, pack, and deliver the early products.</p>
         <p><strong>The growth choice.</strong> Gymshark sent some early products to fitness creators the team already followed. Those creators wore and shared the clothing with their own communities. In 2013, Gymshark also took a major risk by reserving a small space at the BodyPower fitness expo. The event brought athletes, fans, and the company together. A newly developed fitted tracksuit gained strong attention, and online demand rose sharply after the expo.</p>
         <p><strong>The entrepreneurial lesson.</strong> Gymshark did not begin with a giant inventory or a perfect product. The team started with accessible resources, learned from being part of the customer community, and changed its offer when the original clothing did not meet the need. Their firsthand experience was valuable insight, but it was not the same as complete evidence. Orders, creator responses, event reactions, and later product results helped test whether other customers agreed.</p>
        </div><img data-template-id="case-image" src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4164455/pexels-photo-4164455.jpeg" alt="Focused man in a gym getting ready for an exercise routine" class="canva-image w-full rounded-2xl object-cover shadow-lg md:sticky md:top-5" loading="lazy">
       </div>
       <p data-template-id="source-note" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(104, 99, 108)" class="canva-text mt-3">Source note: Adapted for class from the NEIA Entrepreneur Hub Case 7 reading focus and Gymshark’s official story.</p><a data-template-id="source-link" class="canva-link underline break-all" href="https://www.gymshark.com/blog/article/the-official-gymshark-story" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"></a>
       <div data-template-id="mark-panel" class="canva-panel panel orange mt-6">
        <h3 data-template-id="mark-heading" style="font-size:18.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text mb-2">Mark the Case</h3>
        <p data-template-id="mark-directions" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text leading-relaxed">As you read, identify one detail about what customers wanted, one resource the team already had, one piece of evidence beyond the founders’ opinions, and one important unanswered question. Use these observations in your responses.</p>
       </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="reflection-heading">
       <div class="section-title">
        <span class="icon-badge pink" aria-hidden="true"><i data-lucide="message-circle-question-mark"></i></span>
        <h2 id="reflection-heading" data-template-id="reflection-heading" style="font-size:23.9993px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text">Case Reflection</h2>
       </div>
       <p data-template-id="reflection-intro" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text mb-5">Respond in one or two clear sentences. Use at least one specific detail from the reading in each answer.</p>
       <div class="space-y-5">
        <div>
         <label data-template-id="case-q1-label" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text prompt" for="case_q1">1. What customer frustration did Francis and his friends notice, and how did their own experience help them understand it?</label><textarea id="case_q1" name="case_q1" class="field" required></textarea>
        </div>
        <div>
         <label data-template-id="case-q2-label" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text prompt" for="case_q2">2. Which early choice best demonstrates resourcefulness: drop shipping, making clothing by hand, sending products to fitness creators, or attending BodyPower? Defend your choice with evidence.</label><textarea id="case_q2" name="case_q2" class="field" required></textarea>
        </div>
        <div>
         <label data-template-id="case-q3-label" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text prompt" for="case_q3">3. Why was being part of the lifting community useful but not enough by itself to prove that the product would succeed?</label><textarea id="case_q3" name="case_q3" class="field" required></textarea>
        </div>
        <div>
         <label data-template-id="case-q4-label" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text prompt" for="case_q4">4. Write one customer question the team could have asked before investing in a large clothing order. What would the answer help them decide?</label><textarea id="case_q4" name="case_q4" class="field" required></textarea>
        </div>
       </div>
       <div data-template-id="bridge-panel" class="canva-panel panel pink mt-7">
        <h3 data-template-id="bridge-heading" style="font-size:18.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text mb-2">Bridge to the Studio</h3>
        <p data-template-id="bridge-copy" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text leading-relaxed">Gymshark had to make its product understandable and useful to a specific community. Your rebus has a similar challenge: a solver should struggle productively, notice your clues, and eventually understand the idea. Confusion is evidence. Use it to revise.</p>
       </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="studio-heading">
       <div class="section-title">
        <span class="icon-badge" aria-hidden="true"><i data-lucide="shapes"></i></span>
        <h2 id="studio-heading" data-template-id="studio-heading" style="font-size:23.9993px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text">Rebus Studio Challenge</h2>
       </div>
       <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[1fr_190px] gap-5 items-center">
        <p data-template-id="studio-intro" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text leading-relaxed">A rebus represents a word or phrase through position, size, direction, repetition, spacing, or combinations of words and symbols. Make each solution discoverable without simply writing the answer in the puzzle.</p><img data-template-id="studio-image" src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/8386150/pexels-photo-8386150.jpeg" alt="Colorful letters and a puzzle piece arranged on a light background" class="canva-image w-full max-h-44 object-cover rounded-2xl" loading="lazy">
       </div>
       <h3 data-template-id="deliverable-heading" style="font-size:18.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text mt-6 mb-2">Your Deliverable</h3>
       <p data-template-id="deliverable-copy" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text leading-relaxed">Create three original entrepreneurial rebuses: a mindset trait, an entrepreneurial action, and a course concept or original phrase. Include a separate answer for each, record a peer test, and revise at least one puzzle.</p>
       <h3 data-template-id="build-heading" style="font-size:18.9995px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text mt-7 mb-2">Forty-Minute Build</h3>
       <div class="step">
        <span class="step-time">0–5 minutes</span><span>Choose three targets; sketch two tiny layout ideas for each.</span>
       </div>
       <div class="step">
        <span class="step-time">5–27 minutes</span><span>Create three puzzles with words, symbols, simple drawings, or icons.</span>
       </div>
       <div class="step">
        <span class="step-time">27–35 minutes</span><span>Peer test without showing the answers. Listen without coaching.</span>
       </div>
       <div class="step">
        <span class="step-time">35–40 minutes</span><span>Revise at least one puzzle; finish the answer key and revision note.</span>
       </div>
       <div data-template-id="quality-panel" class="canva-panel panel orange mt-6">
        <p data-template-id="quality-copy" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text leading-relaxed">Quality targets: Use at least two intentional visual clues per puzzle. Aim for a classmate to solve each in about 60 seconds. Explain the key clue, and make your revision respond to what your peer actually noticed. Words, letter arrangements, arrows, spacing, and simple symbols are all welcome.</p>
       </div>
      </section>
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="canvas-heading">
       <div class="section-title">
        <span class="icon-badge orange" aria-hidden="true"><i data-lucide="pencil-ruler"></i></span>
        <h2 id="canvas-heading" data-template-id="canvas-heading" style="font-size:23.9993px;font-weight:700;color:rgb(23, 23, 23)" class="canva-text">Creator Canvas</h2>
       </div>
       <p data-template-id="canvas-directions" style="font-size:15.9995px;font-weight:400;color:rgb(36, 36, 36)" class="canva-text